The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2016) and 2449 (2018), and its presidential statements of 15 February 2013, 29 August 2014 and 22 March 2015,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,

Reaffirming also the principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence of humanitarian assistance,

Deeply concerned by the continuing dire humanitarian situation in Syria, including the displacement of millions of people, the lack of access to basic services, and the impact of the conflict on civilians, in particular women and children,

Expressing grave concern at the continuing impediments to the delivery of humanitarian assistance to all those in need, including through cross-line operations, and at attacks against civilians, medical personnel, humanitarian personnel, and medical facilities,

Reaffirming the need for all parties to comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precaution, and to respect and protect medical personnel, humanitarian personnel, and civilian objects,

Recalling the need to ensure that humanitarian assistance reaches all those in need, including those in areas affected by the conflict, and noting the important role of United Nations cross-border operations in this regard,

Noting the reports of the Secretary-General on the implementation of resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2016) and 2449 (2018), and the letters of the Secretary-General of 31 December 2018 and 12 June 2019,

Determining that the humanitarian situation in Syria constitutes a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew the authorization set out in paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of six months, until 10 July 2020, through the border crossings at Bab al-Hawa and Bab al-Salam;

2. Decides that the authorization set out in paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) shall cease to apply to the border crossings at Al Yarubiyah and Al Ramtha on 10 January 2020;

3. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to ensure the effective functioning of the United Nations monitoring mechanism, and to report to the Security Council on a monthly basis on the implementation of this resolution, including on humanitarian access and the progress of the transition from the border crossings at Al Yarubiyah and Al Ramtha;

4. Reaffirms the need for all parties to respect international humanitarian law and human rights law, and to ensure safe, unhindered and sustained access for humanitarian assistance to all those in need, including through cross-line operations; demands that all parties allow voluntary, safe and unhindered passage for humanitarian workers and medical evacuations, and facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance;

5. Demands that all parties immediately cease attacks against civilians, medical personnel, humanitarian personnel, and medical facilities, and respect medical neutrality;

6. Reaffirms that humanitarian assistance should be provided in accordance with the principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, and stresses the need for the United Nations to continue to strengthen monitoring and reporting, including to prevent the diversion of humanitarian assistance to terrorist groups, and reaffirms that counter-terrorism measures should not impede humanitarian action;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to use his good offices to facilitate humanitarian access, including through cross-line operations, and to provide recommendations on how to improve access to all those in need;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

